\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

\looseness=-2
Across six METABENCH benchmarks, 17.9--31.3\% of items exhibit temporal DIF across 2023--2024 model cohorts (${\approx}$27--29\% for MMLU; conservative bound: 13.5\%), while aggregate rankings remain preserved ($\rho \approx 0.997$). Global exposure and differential functioning are complementary diagnostic dimensions (Jaccard $= 0.206$), confirmed by opposite base-vs-instruct DIF patterns ($\chi^2 = 562.7$). DIF-corrected scoring shifts accuracy by ${+}2.12$\,pp on average, with rank displacements exceeding ${\pm}500$. Mechanism-conditional DIF audit should complement contamination screening as standard practice~\citep{habba2026growingpains}.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix~\citep{kipnis2024metabench} (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items~\citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}), with temporal cohorts defined by release date (2023 vs.\ 2024). Cross-benchmark validation across all six METABENCH benchmarks appears in \Cref{sec:gsm8k}.

\subsection{Validation: DIF Detection Power}
\label{sec:validation}

\looseness=-1
A Monte Carlo power analysis (1,000 replications) using empirical 2PL parameters from PSN-IRT~\citep{zhou2026psnirt} ($\bar{a} = 1.2$, $\bar{b} = -0.84$) confirms that at $N \geq 80$ per cohort, ETS~C achieves TPR of 0.68--0.80 and AUC of 0.93--0.94 for $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.4$ (\Cref{app:method_details}). Our temporal cohorts ($N_{\mathrm{ref}} = 1{,}080$, $N_{\mathrm{focal}} = 807$) far exceed this threshold; power varies with item discrimination (TPR 0.40--0.95 across $a = 0.5$--$2.0$; \Cref{app:power_sensitivity}).

\subsection{Temporal DIF: 31\% of Items Exhibit Differential Functioning}
\label{sec:temporal}

\looseness=-1
We compare 2023 ($N = 1{,}080$) against 2024 ($N = 807$) models; 31.3\% of items receive ETS~C classification ($|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.5$; bootstrap 95\% CI: $[29.4\%, 38.1\%]$; family-level cluster bootstrap: $[26.7\%, 53.2\%]$, upper bound driven by mega-family over-representation; \Cref{app:uncertainty}). Two DEFF estimators bound the non-independence effect in parallel: simple-mean DEFF (29.2\%) and cluster bootstrap (26.7\%) converge on ${\approx}$27--29\%, while the conservative weighted-mean DEFF yields 13.5\%---still ${\approx}22\times$ the random baseline (\Cref{app:deff,app:deff_simulation}). We recommend simple-mean DEFF as the primary estimate: it converges with the cluster bootstrap and DEFF simulation, while weighted-mean DEFF over-corrects by down-weighting large families with systematically lower ICC. Under 100 random permutations within the 2023 cohort (540 vs.\ 540), the 99th percentile of item-level $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}|$ is 1.45---just below the ETS~C threshold of 1.5---confirming that the threshold corresponds to a per-item false-positive rate below 1\% in the LLM setting. No single family drives the signal (LOFO $|\Delta\mathrm{C\%}| \leq 2.93$\,pp); graduated de-duplication confirms C\% scales with power, not redundancy (\Cref{app:dedup_k}).

\looseness=-1
Three controls confirm genuine temporal change (\Cref{tab:temporal_landscape}): random splits yield mean C\% of 0.62\% (2023; 100 permutations) and 1.3\% (2024; 100 permutations), both ${\geq}24\times$ below temporal C\%; ability-quintile comparison yields 2.9\%; and 1:1 nearest-neighbor matching on total score ($N{=}638$/group, Cohen's $d = -0.17$) yields C\% $= 28.9\% \pm 0.1\%$, confirming at most ${\sim}2.4$~pp is attributable to ability mismatch ($p < 0.01$; \Cref{app:method_details}).

\looseness=-1
Across all 57 subjects, C\% ranges from 20.7\% to 48.4\% (mean $\approx 30\%$; \Cref{tab:temporal_landscape}). DIF rates are domain-invariant (Kruskal-Wallis $p = 0.78$), but DIF \emph{direction} exhibits domain dependence: STEM items favor 2024 models (55.4\%), Humanities favor 2023 (64.4\%). Domain-level accuracy gains explain ${<}0.1\%$ of item-level DIF variance (pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$); a Simpson's paradox confirms DIF is associated with item properties rather than aggregate gains (OR reverses from 1.55 to 0.73 after controlling for difficulty; \Cref{app:structural}). A difficulty--direction interaction emerges ($\rho = -0.597$): 2024 models gain on hard items while eroding on easy ones. The conclusion is robust across thresholds ($18.3\%$ at $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 2.0$, $10.2\%$ at $\geq 2.5$).

